{
  "about": "K3 economy evals with a real agent (builder/web3/economy/evals/run.mjs): each case a fresh copy of the kit, a game from coin-hop, one headless Claude Code session given the case sentence alone (model: the default), the kit MCP server and skills for that session only, then score.mjs. \"standard\" is the kit as it ships; \"heldOut\" (--holdout) removes the case own reference/fixture and its cases.json entry from the copy first. Live: three held-out economies put live on devnet through economyFlow with the test-creator key standing in for the creator wallet (builder/web3/economy/evals/live.mjs). Transcripts are not committed (about 3 MB a run).",
  "date": "2026-10-11",
  "headline": {
    "standard": "20 of 20",
    "heldOut": [
      "19 of 20",
      "19 of 20"
    ],
    "live": "3 of 3"
  },
  "findings": [
    "As the kit ships, the answer is in reach: economy_examples lists every eval reference and fixture with its prompt (an eval prompt word for word, and an `eval` field naming the case), and in all 20 standard sessions the agent fetched that case own reference by name (3 of 20 saved it unchanged apart from its name, prompt and scenario). So 20 of 20 measures finding the example, not composing an economy. The held-out runs are the fairer number.",
    "Held out, 19 of 20 pass, the same 19 in two runs. e17-stud-farm fails both times on one check (\"breeding pays about 5 to the stud holder\": a recipe input paying to holder:<address>). The program pays a holder only by naming one collectible address; the prompt gives none, no stud exists before going live, and the skill says never guess an address. Both agents said so plainly and chose the nearest thing (fees into a pool, paid out by a decision the creator signs, with per-stud recipes once studs exist). The check needs an address the sentence does not give: a check too strict for the prompt, not a misleading tool. cases.json is frozen (change only by a new case id), so it was left as it is.",
    "e01 coin flip (known ambiguous): no question can be answered in a headless run (the runner tells the session so, and to choose and say what it chose). All three runs kept the reading as written (2% to stakers + 10% to the lottery, 88% back) and told the creator plainly that players keep 88%, not 98%. The case accepts that reading (return 80 to 99%, feeds 1-5% stake and 5-15% lottery); the other reading (98% back, the pot from tickets alone) would fail its lottery-feed check.",
    "No skill or tool text misled an agent in these runs, so nothing in skills/gamesfun-economy or builder/mcp/tools/economy.mjs was changed. Every session saved with economy_save, none called economy_publish (none was asked to put it live; going live needs the creator wallet in the studio page by design). The only denials were the web3 picture tools in the first pilot (now allowed: read-only)."
  ],
